{
 "format": "avgen-scene",
 "version": 1,
 "name": "Lighting Lab",
 "_note": "Flat, fog-free and fixed-seed, so the only thing that varies across the frame is which lights reach a surface and at what angle. Every light comes from examples/lightrigs/lighting-lab.rig.json and every one of them is placed in WORLD coordinates (followCamera false), because the camera is moved in three of the cases and a rig that followed it would move the whole experiment with it. The focal point below is what sizes the rig: (0, 1.5, 0) with radius 4, so 'distance 2.0' means 8 m and every local light's range is 48 m. Without it the rig would be sized from the whole 256 m terrain and every light would sit hundreds of metres out, which is the failure a lighting fixture on top of a world most easily makes. The geometry answers four of the specification's questions and nothing else: normal-ball and the tilt-NN fan are normal response (one surface with every normal, and seven with normals a number can be read off); the rough-NNN and metal-NNN rows are material response at fixed albedo and fixed size; the post row is falloff read as a sequence rather than inferred from one surface; and far-ball at 40 m is outside every local light's 48 m range only by a margin, with far-ball-control beside it as the pair that says a change there is a change and not noise. The three glTF nodes are the Shadow Lab's own assets at the Shadow Lab's own scales, so a finding about a tree here and a finding about a tree there are about the same tree.",
